course: dictionary
lesson: 10301
title: Toughness: strong, brave, and hard to break
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Toughness is a noun, a thing you can have or show. It can mean being strong and hard to break, being brave in your mind when life gets hard, or being hard to chew, like tough meat. «Maddie became captain of the girls' football team, where her toughness was finally useful» shows the brave, strong kind of toughness.
explain: Read the words around toughness to find its meaning. The words «captain» and «football team» tell you this is about strength in sport. Maddie's rule, «we tackle the ball, not people», shows that real toughness does not mean hurting others.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence in place of toughness. "Her strength was finally useful" still makes sense, so strength is a good match. If your meaning does not fit, try another one.
fact: Toughness is a noun: you can have toughness, or show it.
fact: «Toughness» means being strong and hard to break, bend or damage.
fact: «Toughness» can also mean being brave in your mind and able to put up with pain or hard times.
fact: «Toughness» of meat or food means it is hard to cut or chew.
fact: In science, toughness is how much energy a material can take before it breaks.
exercise: «Maddie became captain of the girls' football team, where her toughness was finally useful.» What does toughness mean here? | Being very quiet | *Being strong and brave when things are hard | Being hard to chew | Being late for practice
exercise: Maddie's team rule was «we tackle the ball, not people». What does this rule show about real toughness? | *Real toughness can mean playing hard but never hurting others | Real toughness is only about being loud | Real toughness means never playing at all | Hitting others is the best way to show it
exercise: A scientist tests the toughness of a plastic cup. What is she finding out? | How cold the cup is | What colour the cup is | *How much energy the plastic can take before it breaks | How much the cup costs
test: Which phrase is about being strong in your mind? | A hard-boiled egg | *Mental toughness | Thick cardboard | Toughness of the bread
test: Clubs and cadets give energy and toughness «a useful, safe place to go». What does this suggest about where toughness is best used? | Only in the kitchen | *In safe, useful activities that build strength and courage | Only in fights in the street | Nowhere at all
exam: Which sentence uses toughness to mean hard to chew? | The material passed its toughness test in the lab | *The steak was so tough that Sam could barely chew it | Her toughness helped her finish the marathon | Mental toughness helped the team win
